WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.190 --> 00:00:03.300
If you looked on a globe

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:05.730
and found the most
remote, landlocked point,

00:00:05.730 --> 00:00:07.473
this is where you'd end up.

00:00:08.450 --> 00:00:09.830
And it's near this spot

00:00:09.830 --> 00:00:11.170
where Kazakhstan and China

00:00:11.170 --> 00:00:14.000
have built a giant,
multi-million dollar landport,

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:17.493
economic zone and border
towns entirely from scratch.

00:00:18.850 --> 00:00:21.710
Their goals: to lure
manufacturers to the area

00:00:21.710 --> 00:00:23.230
that might want to take advantage

00:00:23.230 --> 00:00:25.460
of an overland shipping route to Europe,

00:00:25.460 --> 00:00:26.820
establishing Kazakhstan

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:29.500
as a logistics and manufacturing hub.

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:31.720
And to strengthen an economic partnership

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:33.483
that's been decades in the making.

00:00:35.650 --> 00:00:37.060
But on the China side,

00:00:37.060 --> 00:00:39.420
in the heavily policed Xinjiang province,

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:41.670
known for its detention of Muslim Uighurs,

00:00:41.670 --> 00:00:44.503
it isn't proving to be the
ideal business environment.

00:00:46.540 --> 00:00:49.620
In the early '90s people
could travel without visas.

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:51.060
Yeah, but very quickly it finished.

00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:52.400
And now it's difficult

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:55.660
for Chinese to get visas to Kazakhstan,

00:00:55.660 --> 00:00:59.670
and it's difficult for
Kazakhstanis to get visas to China.

00:00:59.670 --> 00:01:01.270
And now with what's happening in Xinjiang,

00:01:01.270 --> 00:01:03.673
it's becoming, you know,
even more problematic.

00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:07.450
So can money alone attract businesses

00:01:07.450 --> 00:01:10.630
to a route so far from the
beaten track of global trade,

00:01:10.630 --> 00:01:12.843
where geopolitical tensions run high?

00:01:21.970 --> 00:01:24.340
Khorgos is on the Chinese-Kazakh border

00:01:24.340 --> 00:01:26.680
near an area that geographers refer to as

00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:29.000
the Eurasian point of inaccessibility.

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:31.250
This is literally the middle of nowhere

00:01:31.250 --> 00:01:33.960
by definition. It's one
of the furthest places

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:35.480
from any major ocean.

00:01:35.480 --> 00:01:40.480
We have satellite imagery
of that area in 2010

00:01:40.750 --> 00:01:41.770
and there's nothing there.

00:01:41.770 --> 00:01:44.040
It's barren steppe.

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:45.630
You flash forward seven years,

00:01:45.630 --> 00:01:48.330
and you see this gleaming
infrastructure that's there.

00:01:49.950 --> 00:01:51.250
If everything goes to plan,

00:01:51.250 --> 00:01:54.120
it will be the largest
dry port in the world,

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:57.430
but the port itself is only
a small piece of the puzzle.

00:01:57.430 --> 00:02:00.370
They have not only new train track

00:02:00.370 --> 00:02:03.700
but also facilities that
are set up to transfer cargo

00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:08.320
from the Chinese gauge
track, which is more narrow,

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:12.140
to the former Soviet
track, which is broader.

00:02:12.140 --> 00:02:15.090
They also have a special economic zone,

00:02:15.090 --> 00:02:16.690
and they've set out an area

00:02:16.690 --> 00:02:17.920
that they hope will be developed

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:19.360
into manufacturing facilities.

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:21.690
And they even have a town
that they're building

00:02:21.690 --> 00:02:24.506
near this that is
supposed to house workers.

00:02:24.506 --> 00:02:28.600
On the China side, a
town of 100,000 people,

00:02:28.600 --> 00:02:31.880
called Korgas, has been
built in just six years.

00:02:31.880 --> 00:02:33.780
These new towns, train routes

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:36.340
and facilities are what
China and Kazakhstan

00:02:36.340 --> 00:02:39.133
hopes is the start of
a major rush out west.

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:44.190
But so far, it's not attracting
clients as they'd hoped.

00:02:44.190 --> 00:02:46.220
Although there has been a steady increase,

00:02:46.220 --> 00:02:48.290
experts say it's due to heavy subsidies

00:02:48.290 --> 00:02:49.830
given by the Chinese government

00:02:49.830 --> 00:02:52.143
to companies that use
the new train routes.

00:02:53.325 --> 00:02:55.940
And were those subsidies to be taken away,

00:02:55.940 --> 00:02:58.320
I think you would see
some of the usage go down

00:02:58.320 --> 00:02:59.430
in those routes.

00:02:59.430 --> 00:03:01.377
We also haven't talked about the fact

00:03:01.377 --> 00:03:04.040
that there's a lot less
coming back from Europe

00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:06.070
just given the trade imbalance.

00:03:06.070 --> 00:03:09.853
And that's a structural
challenge that these routes face.

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:12.730
Because China exports so much more

00:03:12.730 --> 00:03:15.741
than it imports, trains
often return empty.

00:03:15.741 --> 00:03:19.020
And with border crossings,
different rail gauges

00:03:19.020 --> 00:03:22.010
and political issues not
encountered on maritime routes,

00:03:22.010 --> 00:03:24.890
the majority of companies using sea or air

00:03:24.890 --> 00:03:27.320
are unlikely to switch to less predictable

00:03:27.320 --> 00:03:30.573
and significantly more expensive
overland freight shipping.

00:03:34.350 --> 00:03:39.350
So, I think last year,
Khorgos had 160,000 TEU,

00:03:39.430 --> 00:03:43.010
which are, you know,
think of it as containers

00:03:43.010 --> 00:03:44.640
that are moving through there.

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:47.400
And to put that into perspective,

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:50.530
Shanghai, which is the
world's busiest port,

00:03:50.530 --> 00:03:52.970
had 43.3 million.

00:03:52.970 --> 00:03:56.143
270 times what Khorgos is doing.

00:04:00.040 --> 00:04:01.070
Although it doesn't appear

00:04:01.070 --> 00:04:03.100
to be a great investment by China,

00:04:03.100 --> 00:04:05.470
that's not the whole picture.

00:04:05.470 --> 00:04:08.730
Khorgos has become a highly
publicized flagship project

00:04:08.730 --> 00:04:12.690
of Xi Jinping's One Belt,
One Road initiative.

00:04:12.690 --> 00:04:15.720
Promoted as a reopening
of the ancient Silk Road

00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:18.680
that transported goods
overland from China to Europe

00:04:18.680 --> 00:04:20.563
starting from the 2nd century BCE.

00:04:22.220 --> 00:04:23.400
Including Khorgos,

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:27.060
the initiative promises to
invest over US$1 trillion dollars

00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:30.353
in nearly 140 countries and organizations.

00:04:33.050 --> 00:04:36.310
Xi hopes that by extending credit

00:04:36.310 --> 00:04:41.310
and creating a network of
economic dependencies around Asia,

00:04:41.330 --> 00:04:44.560
Africa, Latin America and Europe,

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:47.610
China will also be able
to punch at its weight

00:04:47.610 --> 00:04:49.513
in terms of global influence.

00:04:51.540 --> 00:04:52.860
Critics call this method

00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:55.050
"infrastructure imperialism"

00:04:55.050 --> 00:04:58.000
but it's also been called
"infrastructure diplomacy"

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:00.150
and it's not the first
time it's been used.

00:05:01.930 --> 00:05:06.330
The Roman Empire famously
built roads and highways

00:05:06.330 --> 00:05:11.330
spiraling out from Rome
as a conduit for military

00:05:11.690 --> 00:05:14.830
and commercial activity.

00:05:14.830 --> 00:05:19.339
The U.S. Marshall Plan, in
the years after World War II,

00:05:19.339 --> 00:05:24.339
was an attempt to shore up
Europe to create a bulwark

00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:28.460
against the threat of Soviet imperialism

00:05:28.460 --> 00:05:31.280
and a big part of that was U.S. financing

00:05:31.280 --> 00:05:33.730
for the reconstruction of Europe

00:05:33.730 --> 00:05:36.410
after the devastation of World War II.

00:05:36.410 --> 00:05:38.120
Working together with the aid

00:05:38.120 --> 00:05:39.313
of the Marshall plan,

00:05:40.490 --> 00:05:44.100
the nations of Europe have come
a long way toward recovery.

00:05:44.100 --> 00:05:46.150
And some would argue, it worked.

00:05:46.150 --> 00:05:47.540
The countries were rebuilt,

00:05:47.540 --> 00:05:49.450
and in return sided with the U.S.,

00:05:49.450 --> 00:05:51.863
limiting the influence
of the Soviet Union.

00:05:52.920 --> 00:05:55.009
Where China differs, I think,

00:05:55.009 --> 00:05:58.410
is on the pace and the
scale of the activity.

00:05:58.410 --> 00:06:00.920
The Marshall Plan was big,

00:06:00.920 --> 00:06:03.610
but $15 billion in spending,

00:06:03.610 --> 00:06:08.080
even back in 1948 wasn't that significant.

00:06:08.080 --> 00:06:11.290
China, on even the most modest estimate

00:06:11.290 --> 00:06:13.740
is spending hundreds
of billions of dollars

00:06:13.740 --> 00:06:16.913
and building not just in
Europe, but around the world.

00:06:19.390 --> 00:06:21.910
There's also an economic dimension.

00:06:21.910 --> 00:06:26.430
China has the world's leading
construction companies.

00:06:26.430 --> 00:06:30.320
It has enormous capacity to
produce steel and cement.

00:06:30.320 --> 00:06:34.840
China hopes to export
its production capacity,

00:06:34.840 --> 00:06:39.013
creating new markets and new
profits for its businesses.

00:06:44.970 --> 00:06:47.410
The main question on economists' minds,

00:06:47.410 --> 00:06:49.750
will China's strategy of building first

00:06:49.750 --> 00:06:52.713
and finding demand later
ever lead to real growth?

00:06:55.670 --> 00:06:59.130
China follows a "field of dreams" strategy

00:06:59.130 --> 00:07:01.110
on infrastructure building.

00:07:01.110 --> 00:07:04.030
If we build it, they will come.

00:07:04.030 --> 00:07:07.400
If we go back in time
around 10 or 12 years

00:07:07.400 --> 00:07:10.480
and we think about one of
the main stories in China,

00:07:10.480 --> 00:07:12.970
it was the ghost town narrative.

00:07:12.970 --> 00:07:16.200
China had built enormous
amounts of real estate

00:07:16.200 --> 00:07:18.230
and there was no one living in it.

00:07:18.230 --> 00:07:20.410
And journalists in China traveled around

00:07:20.410 --> 00:07:23.640
and they saw cities in the
desert that were unoccupied,

00:07:23.640 --> 00:07:26.936
tower blocks where new
lights came on at night.

00:07:26.936 --> 00:07:30.420
What happened, though, was
that over a period of years

00:07:30.420 --> 00:07:32.060
with policy support,

00:07:32.060 --> 00:07:35.250
with incomes rising and
with continued urbanization,

00:07:35.250 --> 00:07:38.110
a lot of these ghost towns filled up.

00:07:38.110 --> 00:07:40.970
I think about Zhengzhou, for example,

00:07:40.970 --> 00:07:45.530
a ghost town 10 years ago,
now a thriving urban center.

00:07:45.530 --> 00:07:48.380
Beijing's hope for its
Belt and Road projects

00:07:48.380 --> 00:07:51.340
is that a similar dynamic will play out.

00:07:51.340 --> 00:07:52.750
They built early.

00:07:52.750 --> 00:07:55.940
They provided supply ahead of demand.

00:07:55.940 --> 00:07:57.203
Demand will catch up.

00:07:59.170 --> 00:08:00.910
And China is set to get something else

00:08:00.910 --> 00:08:02.640
out of all this,

00:08:02.640 --> 00:08:04.470
the development of Xinjiang,

00:08:04.470 --> 00:08:06.720
the country's largest
administrative region

00:08:06.720 --> 00:08:08.093
that borders Kazakhstan.

00:08:09.660 --> 00:08:11.950
One of the expressed goals

00:08:11.950 --> 00:08:15.280
for the Belt and Road
expressed by Chinese officials,

00:08:15.280 --> 00:08:17.470
was to develop Western China,

00:08:17.470 --> 00:08:20.240
and it was also to try to address

00:08:20.240 --> 00:08:22.480
some of the inequality that exists

00:08:22.480 --> 00:08:25.520
between Eastern China
and its coastal cities

00:08:25.520 --> 00:08:27.053
and Western China inland.

00:08:30.600 --> 00:08:32.360
That economic disparity,

00:08:32.360 --> 00:08:34.550
along with the government's
repressive policies

00:08:34.550 --> 00:08:36.970
against religious and cultural practices,

00:08:36.970 --> 00:08:40.450
has lead to clashes between the
local Muslim Uighur majority

00:08:40.450 --> 00:08:43.200
and the more well-off Han
Chinese who live in the area.

00:08:44.580 --> 00:08:47.870
By developing the economies
of Xinjiang and Kazakhstan,

00:08:47.870 --> 00:08:49.986
which share many cultural similarities,

00:08:49.986 --> 00:08:53.390
Chinese policy makers hope
to put an end to unrest

00:08:53.390 --> 00:08:54.223
in the region.

00:08:56.200 --> 00:08:59.290
The same communities live
on both sides of the border.

00:08:59.290 --> 00:09:01.820
We have a lot of Uighurs in Kazakhstan,

00:09:01.820 --> 00:09:03.200
we have a lot of Kazakhs,

00:09:03.200 --> 00:09:05.690
one million Kazakhs living in Xinjiang.

00:09:05.690 --> 00:09:07.250
So there are these connections.

00:09:07.250 --> 00:09:08.760
And Uighurs in Xinjiang,

00:09:08.760 --> 00:09:12.190
they have family links
with Kazakh citizens.

00:09:12.190 --> 00:09:13.370
We are Muslims.

00:09:13.370 --> 00:09:15.960
We eat the same food.

00:09:15.960 --> 00:09:19.573
So there are important and deep linkages.

00:09:22.550 --> 00:09:24.830
Xinjiang has a large Kazakh community,

00:09:24.830 --> 00:09:27.170
about 1.6 million people,

00:09:27.170 --> 00:09:29.830
while Kazakhstan has a
large Uighur diaspora

00:09:29.830 --> 00:09:33.133
with a population of around 261,000.

00:09:34.200 --> 00:09:36.290
This link could be an incentive for China

00:09:36.290 --> 00:09:39.000
to develop the region
and calm ethnic tensions

00:09:39.000 --> 00:09:40.963
by investing money in Kazakhstan.

00:09:42.860 --> 00:09:44.240
The main goal is to become

00:09:44.240 --> 00:09:46.280
one of the 30 most developed countries,

00:09:46.280 --> 00:09:49.110
which implies modernizing the economy,

00:09:49.110 --> 00:09:52.150
upgrading the economy,
diversifying it for sure.

00:09:52.150 --> 00:09:54.210
I would say quite a few
projects wouldn't be possible

00:09:54.210 --> 00:09:55.043
without China.

00:09:55.043 --> 00:09:56.570
Not only without Chinese money,

00:09:56.570 --> 00:09:58.140
but Chinese kind of belief

00:09:58.140 --> 00:10:00.903
in the usefulness of these projects.

00:10:12.230 --> 00:10:14.270
A year after the program was announced,

00:10:14.270 --> 00:10:16.390
President Nursultan Nazarbayev,

00:10:16.390 --> 00:10:18.530
announced their own infrastructure project

00:10:18.530 --> 00:10:19.910
called "Bright Path."

00:10:19.910 --> 00:10:21.560
This project works in tandem

00:10:21.560 --> 00:10:23.580
with the Belt and Road Initiative,

00:10:23.580 --> 00:10:26.950
a $9 billion domestic
economic stimulus plan

00:10:26.950 --> 00:10:31.070
to develop and modernize
roads, railways, ports,

00:10:31.070 --> 00:10:35.000
IT infrastructure, education
and civil services.

00:10:35.000 --> 00:10:38.160
And is necessary to lessen the
country's dependence on oil,

00:10:38.160 --> 00:10:40.803
which has seen a sharp decline since 2014.

00:10:43.500 --> 00:10:46.100
It has this natural resource wealth

00:10:46.100 --> 00:10:49.160
that is not going to exist forever.

00:10:49.160 --> 00:10:52.110
And I think in order to
make the most of that,

00:10:52.110 --> 00:10:56.160
they need to make investments
with an eye toward the future

00:10:56.160 --> 00:10:58.940
and what kind of economy
do they want to have?

00:10:58.940 --> 00:11:01.620
And I think they want more services,

00:11:01.620 --> 00:11:03.160
a little more manufacturing.

00:11:03.160 --> 00:11:06.470
All of those are goals
that you see in small ways

00:11:06.470 --> 00:11:08.020
in the planning around Khorgos.

00:11:10.892 --> 00:11:13.300
But it hasn't come without resistance.

00:11:13.300 --> 00:11:15.680
The shift towards China,
and away from Russia,

00:11:15.680 --> 00:11:18.400
has played into deep
anti-Chinese sentiments

00:11:18.400 --> 00:11:21.093
and fears of China that
date back hundreds of years.

00:11:22.010 --> 00:11:24.400
Kazakh nationalists
complain that their country,

00:11:24.400 --> 00:11:26.670
having gained independence from Moscow,

00:11:26.670 --> 00:11:29.173
now risks becoming a satellite of Beijing.

00:11:30.640 --> 00:11:31.920
And these fears,

00:11:31.920 --> 00:11:35.180
they are linked to the
territorial disputes

00:11:36.110 --> 00:11:38.370
that we had historically.

00:11:38.370 --> 00:11:40.543
In some Chinese textbooks,

00:11:41.540 --> 00:11:42.383
parts of the territory of Kazakhstan

00:11:42.383 --> 00:11:46.260
are considered part of
like historical China.

00:11:46.260 --> 00:11:47.210
And in fact,

00:11:47.210 --> 00:11:51.523
there was kind of this dual
jurisdiction of Kazakh lands.

00:11:53.590 --> 00:11:54.650
When the Kazakh government

00:11:54.650 --> 00:11:56.850
announced legislation in 2017

00:11:56.850 --> 00:11:59.300
to let foreigners rent
land for long periods,

00:11:59.300 --> 00:12:00.830
protesters took to the streets

00:12:00.830 --> 00:12:02.190
to denounce what many saw

00:12:02.190 --> 00:12:04.233
as the start of a Chinese land grab.

00:12:06.470 --> 00:12:09.610
To make matters worse, worries
have grown in recent years

00:12:09.610 --> 00:12:12.173
as China has built a
sprawling surveillance state

00:12:12.173 --> 00:12:14.180
and internment-camp system

00:12:14.180 --> 00:12:18.430
to target its Turkic-Muslim
population, especially Uighurs.

00:12:19.310 --> 00:12:23.260
But now, over the past couple of years,

00:12:23.260 --> 00:12:27.320
Kazakhs also found themselves
inside these camps.

00:12:27.320 --> 00:12:28.880
So the public opinion

00:12:29.880 --> 00:12:32.380
got even more concerned with
what's happening in China

00:12:32.380 --> 00:12:35.250
and what they're doing to our co-ethnics.

00:12:35.250 --> 00:12:36.770
It's unclear how many people

00:12:36.770 --> 00:12:38.740
are in some sort of detention,

00:12:38.740 --> 00:12:39.837
but the U.S. State Department

00:12:39.837 --> 00:12:41.980
estimates more than 1 million people

00:12:41.980 --> 00:12:44.113
have been detained since 2017.

00:12:45.480 --> 00:12:48.250
China, who at first denied
the existence of the camps,

00:12:48.250 --> 00:12:50.430
is now doubling down on the need for them,

00:12:50.430 --> 00:12:51.640
and beginning to defend them

00:12:51.640 --> 00:12:54.180
as a vital weapon against terrorism.

00:12:54.180 --> 00:12:56.010
This has led the U.S. and EU

00:12:56.010 --> 00:12:57.670
to increase sanctions on companies

00:12:57.670 --> 00:12:59.143
who operate in the region.

00:13:00.850 --> 00:13:03.250
I do think that Western China

00:13:03.250 --> 00:13:05.740
exposes what is really a big contradiction

00:13:05.740 --> 00:13:07.100
in the Belt and Road.

00:13:07.100 --> 00:13:08.740
You know, the Belt and Road is advertised

00:13:08.740 --> 00:13:11.610
as something that's supposed
to increase connectivity

00:13:11.610 --> 00:13:13.123
between China and the world.

00:13:16.458 --> 00:13:18.080
♪ The Belt connects the land ♪

00:13:18.080 --> 00:13:20.051
♪ The Road moves on the sea ♪

00:13:20.051 --> 00:13:22.162
♪ The promise that they hold ♪

00:13:22.162 --> 00:13:24.222
♪ Is joint prosperity ♪

00:13:24.222 --> 00:13:25.957
You go into Western China

00:13:25.957 --> 00:13:27.614
and there are security
checkpoints everywhere.

00:13:27.614 --> 00:13:32.550
And, you know, you have
minorities who are being detained.

00:13:32.550 --> 00:13:35.930
It's abhorrent from an ethical standpoint,

00:13:35.930 --> 00:13:39.130
but it's also, in very practical terms,

00:13:39.130 --> 00:13:41.400
a horrible business environment.

00:13:41.400 --> 00:13:42.380
And so, you know,

00:13:42.380 --> 00:13:45.800
China has talked about
increasing connectivity

00:13:45.800 --> 00:13:47.610
and yet in this area,

00:13:47.610 --> 00:13:49.460
it's putting up barriers

00:13:49.460 --> 00:13:51.390
not only for the flow of goods and people,

00:13:51.390 --> 00:13:52.900
but also for data.

00:13:52.900 --> 00:13:54.470
The difficult business environment

00:13:54.470 --> 00:13:56.290
may be why Khorgos hasn't been met

00:13:56.290 --> 00:13:59.230
with the enthusiasm
governments had hoped for.

00:13:59.230 --> 00:14:01.230
And with the China-U.S. trade war,

00:14:01.230 --> 00:14:03.740
a controversial security law in Hong Kong

00:14:03.740 --> 00:14:05.280
and the global pandemic,

00:14:05.280 --> 00:14:07.780
China is signaling a
tougher stance elsewhere

00:14:07.780 --> 00:14:08.863
on the Belt and Road.

00:14:10.347 --> 00:14:13.120
The $62 billion Chinese-Pakistan
Economic Corridor

00:14:13.120 --> 00:14:14.210
has been scaled back

00:14:14.210 --> 00:14:16.450
amid Pakistan's increasing debt problems,

00:14:16.450 --> 00:14:18.780
while a major port deal in Myanmar

00:14:18.780 --> 00:14:22.553
was slimmed down from roughly
$7 billion to $1.3 billion.

00:14:25.190 --> 00:14:27.340
I think we're not really going to know

00:14:27.340 --> 00:14:28.620
how many white elephants

00:14:28.620 --> 00:14:32.310
are roaming along the Belt
and Road, until a bit longer.

00:14:32.310 --> 00:14:36.070
But we are now increasingly
in an economic environment

00:14:36.070 --> 00:14:37.380
where some of that, I expect,

00:14:37.380 --> 00:14:39.373
is gonna start bubbling to the surface.

00:14:41.860 --> 00:14:43.350
At this point it's unclear

00:14:43.350 --> 00:14:45.310
how much China is willing to spend

00:14:45.310 --> 00:14:47.900
to make the Khorgos dream a reality.

00:14:47.900 --> 00:14:51.540
Twenty years ago, it was
America, it was Europe,

00:14:51.540 --> 00:14:52.850
it was the World Bank

00:14:52.850 --> 00:14:56.110
that was funding infrastructure
development spending

00:14:56.110 --> 00:14:57.660
around the world.

00:14:57.660 --> 00:15:01.136
As the West has been rocked
by a series of crises

00:15:01.136 --> 00:15:04.350
and shifted the focus
of their economic policy

00:15:04.350 --> 00:15:07.630
back towards problems in
their domestic economy,

00:15:07.630 --> 00:15:10.120
a huge gap has been opened up,

00:15:10.120 --> 00:15:14.160
a huge infrastructure
funding and expertise gap.

00:15:14.160 --> 00:15:17.640
China is now moving into that space

00:15:17.640 --> 00:15:19.980
and on a project-by-project basis,

00:15:19.980 --> 00:15:23.110
there are successes
and there are failures.

00:15:23.110 --> 00:15:24.570
The larger picture, though,

00:15:24.570 --> 00:15:27.560
is that as China's
dollars continue to flow,

00:15:27.560 --> 00:15:30.290
as China's concrete continues to pour,

00:15:30.290 --> 00:15:33.223
China's influence continues to increase.

